   13	/**
   14	 * The building this planet still needs before the next thing the account wants to produce is
   15	 * possible at all.
   16	 *
   17	 * A seeded account owns nothing, so without this the account grows resources forever and never owns
   18	 * a laboratory or a shipyard -- which is how six of its capabilities became permanently unreachable.
   19	 * The chain is what connects the account's ambitions to the buildings that gate them.
   20	 *
   21	 * Nothing here names an object. The ambitions are whatever the host offers as research or as a unit,
   22	 * and the requirements are the host's own recursive requirement graph, so a module or an expansion
   23	 * that adds a ship, a technology or a building is part of the plan the moment the host knows about
   24	 * it -- and a host that changes a prerequisite changes this plan with no edit here.
   25	 *
   26	 * The module's only policy is the order, and it is the level the host asks for: the easiest unlock
   27	 * first. A player picks up the small prerequisites as they go, and ordering by the requirement
   28	 * rather than by the building is what keeps the plan from rushing the largest shipyard in the game
   29	 * while the account still has no laboratory. Only the level the host asks for counts, because level
   30	 * one of a robotics factory never unlocks a shipyard and treating "something is built" as done would
   31	 * stall the chain one step below its goal.
   32	 *
   33	 * The account works on one ambition at a time: the cheapest thing it cannot yet produce. Satisfying
   34	 * the union of every ambition's prerequisites instead is a ladder with no top -- the catalogue always
   35	 * names one more deep unlock, so the chain never finishes, the economy never gets a turn, and the
   36	 * account ends the day with a level-seven shipyard standing over level-three mines. A player picks a
   37	 * goal, stands what it needs, and goes back to mining; the next goal gets its turn on a later
   38	 * session, and the economy ranks in between.
   39	 *
   40	 * A step the planet cannot pay for because it produces none of a resource the step costs is blocked
   41	 * the same way a missing level is, so the producer of that resource is a chain step too: a player
   42	 * mines what they are short of, and the synthesizer stands before the robotics factory exactly the
   43	 * way the laboratory does.
   44	 *
   45	 * A step is whatever queue accepts it: the host's catalogue holds buildings and technologies beside
   46	 * each other, and a technology gating a laboratory is as much a prerequisite as the laboratory
   47	 * itself. Which queue takes the step is the planner's question, answered from the host's object type.
   48	 */
